00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:00.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  criminals wanting to get their hands on  your stimulus money have resulted in  unprecedented scams from phony phone  calls tax even fake checks  all related to your stimulus money  kirsten joyce is on your side with tips  to avoid having your money getting into  the wrong hands  brian the ftc has received more than 340  000 reports of fraud relating to  coronavirus costing victims more than  320 million dollars to date  socialcatfish.com released the top  stimulus check scams and tips on how to  avoid them based on information it  gathered from the ftc  fbi and the irs now the first is a  robocall check scam and a person will be  calling pretending to be the irs asking  for your personal financial information  they'll claim they need it to deposit  the check into your account  do not give out personal information  scammers claiming they are from the  government may email you  tell you to click on a link to verify  information  or send a text message for a link to  fill out an application to receive your  check  do not click on any links that you don't  recognize  if you click on a suspicious link  they'll likely take you to a fake  website that can steal your information  so there are no  non-government websites giving stimulus  checks  and finally scammers have been mailing  fake checks that look exactly like the  official government-issued paper  stimulus checks  somebody calls you and says hey um  either we've sent you a check or we're  sending you a check  and once once you deposit it they say it  was for too much and we need the money  back or we're gonna  you know you know get it back and so  they have you send them you know the  overage which you know hundred dollars  couple hundred dollars couple thousand  dollars depending what it is  and you you paying that money the check  bounces and then you get in trouble  from your bank for depositing a bad  check so have your bank verify it  david mcclellan is the president of the  website socialcatfish.com he says you  can use a site like his to verify all  the information as these claims  and scams do evolve over time and if you  encounter a scam contact local law  enforcement or file a complaint with the  ftc  for more information on the status of  your check you can also visit the irs  website

